now to some potentially encouraging news  for parents who have struggled to find  baby formula months after the fda shut  down an abbott plant in michigan that  factory has been cleared to reopen  however it could take weeks to restart  production and get formula back on  shelves and there are still many  questions about the biden  administration's response to this crisis  ed o'keefe is at the white house for us  ed good morning to you tony good to see  you that michigan plant was shut down in  february when the company had to begin  recalling product and since then more  than 40 percent of formula has gone  missing off of shelves at major  retailers across the country quite a  shortage so now under an agreement with  the fda abbott can begin the process of  reopening once the agency approves the  initial steps abbott says the factory  can be completely up and running within  two weeks but it would be another six to  eight weeks before formula is back on  the shelves nobody is setting a  definitive date the government or the  company at the fda but if you do the  math it seems the earliest the baby  formula supply would be back to normal  is at least mid-july  the fda also plans to relax some  regulations to allow more formula to be  imported into the united states to boost  supply meantime lawmakers in both  parties among them new york democratic  senator kirsten gillibrand are calling  on president biden to use the defense  production act to compel other companies  to help produce more formula that was  the same law that was used to boost  production of ventilators and other  supplies at the beginning of the  pandemic the white house says it's one  of many options still under  consideration the fda commissioner will  testify on capitol hill later this week  he's likely to face all sorts of  questions including why his agency  waited so long to reopen that michigan  plant given that an investigation found  that the illness traced to this formula  was never actually found at that  facility  nate  ed thank you
 

